<section xml:id="sec-mod7-study-guide">
  <title>Companion Reading and Study Guide</title>
  <p>Reading: Zorn, Sections 4.1<ndash/>4.3. Estimated time: 75 minutes.</p>
    <p>
      This guide accompanies three sections of Zorn's <em>Understanding
      Real Analysis</em>. Section 4.1 (<q>Derivatives</q>) introduces
      the formal limit definition and establishes differentiability as
      a condition stronger than continuity. Section 4.2
      (<q>Differentiation Rules</q>) develops the standard rules as
      theorems proved from the definition. Section 4.3 (<q>The Mean
      Value Theorem</q>) builds from Rolle's Theorem to the MVT and
      applies it to prove monotonicity results and Taylor's theorem.
    </p>
    <p>
      Read each section before working its questions. The theme running
      through all three sections is that <em>every statement about
      derivatives is ultimately a statement about limits</em>, and every
      proof uses the limit machinery from
      <xref ref="ch-mod4" text="custom">Module 4</xref> and
      <xref ref="ch-mod6" text="custom">Module 6</xref>. When you
      encounter a rule you learned mechanically in calculus, ask: which
      limit law is doing the work here?
    </p>
  <!-- ============================================================
       SECTION 4.1: THE DERIVATIVE
  ============================================================ -->

  <paragraphs xml:id="par-mod7-sg-41-intro">
    <title>Section 4.1: The Derivative (Zorn pp. 163&#x2013;172)</title>
    <p>
      Read Zorn's definition of differentiability at a point carefully.
      The difference quotient <m>(f(x) - f(a))/(x - a)</m> is a
      function of <m>x</m>; the derivative is the limit of this function
      as <m>x \to a</m>. Make sure you can translate between the
      <m>x \to a</m> form and the <m>h \to 0</m> form:
      <m>f'(a) = \lim_{h \to 0} (f(a+h) - f(a))/h</m>.
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod7-sg-41-1">
    <title>The Difference Quotient</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Write the difference quotient for <m>f</m> at a point <m>a</m>
        in both the <m>h \to 0</m> and the <m>x \to a</m> form. Then,
        for <m>f(x) = x^2</m>, compute the difference quotient at
        <m>a = 3</m> in simplified form and evaluate its limit.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        The two forms are <m>(f(a+h) - f(a))/h</m> as <m>h \to 0</m>
        and <m>(f(x) - f(a))/(x - a)</m> as <m>x \to a</m>. For
        <m>f(x) = x^2</m> at <m>a = 3</m>:
        <md>\frac{(3+h)^2 - 9}{h} = \frac{9 + 6h + h^2 - 9}{h}
        = \frac{6h + h^2}{h} = 6 + h \to 6 \quad \text{as } h \to 0.</md>
        So <m>f'(3) = 6</m>.
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod7-sg-41-2">
    <title>Differentiability Implies Continuity</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        <ol marker="(a)">
          <li>
            State the definition of differentiability of <m>f</m> at
            a point <m>a</m>.
          </li>
          <li>
            Prove: if <m>f</m> is differentiable at <m>a</m>, then
            <m>f</m> is continuous at <m>a</m>. Write the proof in
            full. The key algebraic step is to write
            <m>f(x) - f(a) = \dfrac{f(x) - f(a)}{x - a} \cdot (x - a)</m>
            and apply the product rule for limits as <m>x \to a</m>.
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        (a) <m>f</m> is differentiable at <m>a</m> if
        <m>\lim_{h \to 0} (f(a+h) - f(a))/h</m> exists (as a finite
        real number); its value is called <m>f'(a)</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        (b) Suppose <m>f</m> is differentiable at <m>a</m>. For
        <m>x \neq a</m>, write
        <md>f(x) - f(a) = \frac{f(x) - f(a)}{x - a} \cdot (x - a).</md>
        As <m>x \to a</m>, the first factor tends to <m>f'(a)</m>
        (by differentiability) and the second factor tends to <m>0</m>.
        By the product rule for limits,
        <m>f(x) - f(a) \to f'(a) \cdot 0 = 0</m>,
        so <m>\lim_{x \to a} f(x) = f(a)</m>, confirming continuity.
        <m>\square</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod7-sg-41-3">
    <title>The Sum Rule from the Definition</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Prove from the limit definition that
        <m>(f + g)'(a) = f'(a) + g'(a)</m> (the sum rule), assuming
        both <m>f</m> and <m>g</m> are differentiable at <m>a</m>.
        Your proof must explicitly write the difference quotient for
        <m>f + g</m>, split it into two separate difference quotients,
        and invoke the sum rule for limits.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        The difference quotient for <m>f + g</m> at <m>a</m> is
        <md>
          <mrow>\frac{(f+g)(a+h) - (f+g)(a)}{h}
          \amp= \frac{[f(a+h) - f(a)] + [g(a+h) - g(a)]}{h}</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp= \frac{f(a+h) - f(a)}{h} + \frac{g(a+h) - g(a)}{h}.</mrow>
        </md>
        As <m>h \to 0</m>, the first term tends to <m>f'(a)</m> and
        the second tends to <m>g'(a)</m>, by differentiability of
        <m>f</m> and <m>g</m> at <m>a</m>. By the sum rule for limits,
        <m>(f+g)'(a) = f'(a) + g'(a)</m>. <m>\square</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ============================================================
       SECTION 4.2: DIFFERENTIATION RULES
  ============================================================ -->

  <paragraphs xml:id="par-mod7-sg-42-intro">
    <title>Section 4.2: Differentiation Rules (Zorn pp. 172&#x2013;182)</title>
    <p>
      Section 4.2 develops the product rule, quotient rule, and chain
      rule as theorems. The product rule proof is a model of the
      <q>add and subtract a clever term</q> technique that appears
      repeatedly in analysis. The chain rule proof requires more care
      because of a subtle division-by-zero issue.
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod7-sg-42-1">
    <title>The Product Rule</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        State the product rule: if <m>f</m> and <m>g</m> are
        differentiable at <m>a</m>, then <m>fg</m> is differentiable
        at <m>a</m> and <m>(fg)'(a) = f'(a)g(a) + f(a)g'(a)</m>.
        Then prove it from the limit definition. The key step is to
        add and subtract the term <m>f(a+h)g(a)</m> in the numerator
        of the difference quotient for <m>fg</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        The difference quotient for <m>fg</m> at <m>a</m> is
        <md>\frac{f(a+h)g(a+h) - f(a)g(a)}{h}.</md>
        Add and subtract <m>f(a+h)g(a)</m>:
        <md>
          <mrow>\frac{f(a+h)g(a+h) - f(a)g(a)}{h}
          \amp= \frac{f(a+h)g(a+h) - f(a+h)g(a) + f(a+h)g(a) - f(a)g(a)}{h}</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp= f(a+h) \cdot \frac{g(a+h) - g(a)}{h}
                   + g(a) \cdot \frac{f(a+h) - f(a)}{h}.</mrow>
        </md>
        As <m>h \to 0</m>: <m>f(a+h) \to f(a)</m> (since <m>f</m> is
        continuous at <m>a</m>, by differentiability), the factor
        <m>(g(a+h) - g(a))/h</m> tends to <m>g'(a)</m>, and the second
        term is the constant <m>g(a)</m> times the factor
        <m>(f(a+h) - f(a))/h</m>, which tends to <m>f'(a)</m>. By the
        sum and product rules for limits, the difference quotient tends
        to <m>f(a)g'(a) + g(a)f'(a)</m>, so <m>fg</m> is differentiable
        at <m>a</m> and <m>(fg)'(a) = f(a)g'(a) + g(a)f'(a)</m>.
        <m>\square</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod7-sg-42-2">
    <title>The Chain Rule and Its Proof</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        <ol marker="(a)">
          <li>
            State the chain rule: if <m>f</m> is differentiable at
            <m>a</m> and <m>g</m> is differentiable at <m>f(a)</m>,
            then <m>(g \circ f)'(a) = g'(f(a)) \cdot f'(a)</m>.
          </li>
          <li>
            Explain in words why the <q>obvious</q> proof attempt&#x2014;multiply
            and divide by <m>f(a+h) - f(a)</m>&#x2014;has a gap.
          </li>
          <li>
            Describe the auxiliary function approach that fixes the gap.
            Define <m>\psi(k) = (g(f(a)+k) - g(f(a)))/k - g'(f(a))</m>
            for <m>k \neq 0</m> and <m>\psi(0) = 0</m>, and explain
            why this function is continuous at <m>0</m>.
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        (a) The chain rule statement is given. (b) The obvious approach
        writes
        <md>\frac{g(f(a+h)) - g(f(a))}{h}
        = \frac{g(f(a+h)) - g(f(a))}{f(a+h) - f(a)}
          \cdot \frac{f(a+h) - f(a)}{h},</md>
        intending to take limits to get <m>g'(f(a)) \cdot f'(a)</m>.
        The gap: <m>f(a+h) - f(a)</m> might equal <m>0</m> for
        arbitrarily small <m>h \neq 0</m>, making the first fraction
        undefined at those values of <m>h</m>. (c) The auxiliary
        function <m>\psi</m> rewrites <m>g(f(a)+k) - g(f(a)) =
        (g'(f(a)) + \psi(k)) k</m> for all <m>k</m>, including
        <m>k = 0</m>. Substituting <m>k = f(a+h) - f(a)</m> gives
        a valid expression with no division by zero. Since <m>g</m>
        is differentiable at <m>f(a)</m>, <m>\psi(k) \to 0</m> as
        <m>k \to 0</m>, and <m>\psi</m> is continuous at <m>0</m>.
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod7-sg-42-3">
    <title>Applying the Rules</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Apply the product and chain rules to differentiate each function.
        State which rule you use at each step.
        <ol marker="(a)">
          <li>
            <m>f(x) = x^2 \sin(x)</m>
          </li>
          <li>
            <m>g(x) = (x^2 + 1)^{10}</m>
          </li>
          <li>
            <m>h(x) = \ln(x^2 + 1)</m>, assuming <m>\dfrac{d}{dx}\ln(x) = \dfrac{1}{x}</m>.
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        (a) Product rule: <m>f'(x) = 2x\sin(x) + x^2\cos(x)</m>.
        (b) Chain rule: <m>g'(x) = 10(x^2+1)^9 \cdot 2x = 20x(x^2+1)^9</m>.
        (c) Chain rule: <m>h'(x) = \dfrac{1}{x^2+1} \cdot 2x = \dfrac{2x}{x^2+1}</m>.
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ============================================================
       SECTION 4.3: THE MEAN VALUE THEOREM
  ============================================================ -->

  <paragraphs xml:id="par-mod7-sg-43-intro">
    <title>Section 4.3: The Mean Value Theorem (Zorn pp. 182&#x2013;196)</title>
    <p>
      Section 4.3 is the heart of Module 7. Read the proof of Rolle's
      Theorem carefully: it uses the Extreme Value Theorem (Module 6)
      in an essential way. The MVT proof derives from Rolle's by a
      geometric trick (subtracting the secant line) that you should
      be able to reconstruct from memory by the end of this module.
      The guide then closes with three further results, each stated and
      proved here: Taylor's Theorem, the Cauchy Mean Value Theorem, and
      L'H&#xf4;pital's rule. The Practice Set uses Taylor's Theorem and
      L'H&#xf4;pital's rule, and the Cauchy Mean Value Theorem is the
      step in the proof of L'H&#xf4;pital's rule.
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod7-sg-43-1">
    <title>Rolle's Theorem</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        State Rolle's Theorem in full (all three hypotheses and the
        conclusion). Then, for <m>f(x) = x^2 - 4x</m> on
        <m>[0, 4]</m>, verify that all three hypotheses hold and find
        the value <m>c \in (0, 4)</m> guaranteed by the conclusion.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        Rolle's Theorem: if <m>f</m> is continuous on <m>[a, b]</m>,
        differentiable on <m>(a, b)</m>, and <m>f(a) = f(b)</m>, then
        there exists <m>c \in (a, b)</m> with <m>f'(c) = 0</m>.
        For <m>f(x) = x^2 - 4x</m> on <m>[0, 4]</m>: (i) <m>f</m>
        is a polynomial, hence continuous on <m>[0, 4]</m> and
        differentiable on <m>(0, 4)</m>. (ii) <m>f(0) = 0</m> and
        <m>f(4) = 16 - 16 = 0</m>, so <m>f(0) = f(4)</m>. By
        Rolle's, there exists <m>c \in (0,4)</m> with <m>f'(c) = 0</m>.
        Since <m>f'(x) = 2x - 4</m>, we get <m>c = 2 \in (0, 4)</m>.
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod7-sg-43-2">
    <title>Proof of the MVT from Rolle's Theorem</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        State the Mean Value Theorem. Then prove it using Rolle's
        Theorem by defining the auxiliary function
        <md>h(x) = f(x) - \left[f(a) + \frac{f(b) - f(a)}{b - a}(x - a)\right].</md>
        Verify that <m>h(a) = h(b) = 0</m>, apply Rolle's Theorem to
        <m>h</m>, and interpret the conclusion for <m>f</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        MVT: If <m>f</m> is continuous on <m>[a, b]</m> and
        differentiable on <m>(a, b)</m>, then there exists
        <m>c \in (a, b)</m> such that <m>f'(c) = (f(b) - f(a))/(b - a)</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Proof: Define <m>h(x) = f(x) - [f(a) + \frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a}(x-a)]</m>.
        Then <m>h</m> is continuous on <m>[a, b]</m> and differentiable
        on <m>(a, b)</m> (being a difference of such functions), and
        <m>h(a) = f(a) - f(a) = 0</m>, <m>h(b) = f(b) - [f(a) + (f(b)-f(a))] = 0</m>.
        By Rolle's Theorem, there exists <m>c \in (a, b)</m> with
        <m>h'(c) = 0</m>. Since <m>h'(x) = f'(x) - (f(b)-f(a))/(b-a)</m>,
        we get <m>f'(c) = (f(b)-f(a))/(b-a)</m>. <m>\square</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod7-sg-43-3">
    <title>MVT Application: Zero Derivative</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Use the MVT to prove: if <m>f'(x) = 0</m> for all
        <m>x \in (a, b)</m>, then <m>f</m> is constant on <m>(a, b)</m>.
        (Hint: take any two points <m>x, y \in (a, b)</m> with
        <m>x \lt y</m>, apply the MVT on <m>[x, y]</m>, and use
        <m>f'(c) = 0</m> for the resulting <m>c</m>.)
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        Let <m>x, y \in (a, b)</m> with <m>x \lt y</m>. Since
        <m>f</m> is differentiable on <m>(a, b)</m>, it is continuous
        on <m>[x, y]</m> and differentiable on <m>(x, y)</m>. By the
        MVT, there exists <m>c \in (x, y) \subset (a, b)</m> such that
        <m>f(y) - f(x) = f'(c)(y - x) = 0 \cdot (y - x) = 0</m>.
        Hence <m>f(y) = f(x)</m>. Since <m>x, y \in (a, b)</m> were
        arbitrary, <m>f</m> is constant on <m>(a, b)</m>. <m>\square</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <paragraphs xml:id="par-mod7-sg-43-taylor">
    <title>Taylor's Theorem</title>
    <p>
      The MVT says that <m>f(x) = f(a) + f'(c)(x - a)</m> for some
      <m>c</m> between <m>a</m> and <m>x</m>: it approximates <m>f</m>
      near <m>a</m> by a constant and expresses the error using the
      first derivative at an unknown point. Taylor's Theorem approximates <m>f</m> by a polynomial
      of degree at most <m>n</m> and expresses the error with the
      derivative of order <m>n + 1</m>. This guide states and proves it
      here, together with the Cauchy Mean Value Theorem and
      L'H&#xf4;pital's rule below, because the Practice Set uses
      Taylor's Theorem and L'H&#xf4;pital's rule, and the proof of the
      rule rests on the Cauchy Mean Value Theorem. The proof is one application of Rolle's
      Theorem to a well-chosen auxiliary function, the same idea as the
      proof of the MVT in <xref ref="ex-mod7-sg-43-2"/>.
    </p>
    <definition xml:id="def-mod7-taylor-poly">
      <title>Taylor Polynomial</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>n \geq 0</m> be an integer, and suppose <m>f</m> has
          derivatives <m>f', f'', \ldots, f^{(n)}</m> at <m>a</m> (with
          <m>f^{(0)} = f</m>). The <term>Taylor polynomial of order
          <m>n</m></term> of <m>f</m> at <m>a</m> is
          <md>T_n(x) = \sum_{k=0}^{n} \frac{f^{(k)}(a)}{k!}(x - a)^k
          = f(a) + f'(a)(x - a) + \frac{f''(a)}{2!}(x - a)^2 + \cdots
          + \frac{f^{(n)}(a)}{n!}(x - a)^n,</md>
          and the <term>remainder</term> is <m>R_n(x) = f(x) - T_n(x)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>
    <theorem xml:id="thm-mod7-taylor">
      <title>Taylor's Theorem (Lagrange Form of the Remainder)</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let
          <m>n \geq 0</m> be an integer and let <m>I</m> be an open
          interval. Suppose <m>f</m> is <m>n + 1</m> times differentiable on
          <m>I</m>; that is, <m>f, f', \ldots, f^{(n)}</m> are all
          differentiable at every point of <m>I</m>. Let <m>a, x \in I</m>.
          Then there is a point <m>c</m> between <m>a</m> and <m>x</m> such
          that
          <md>R_n(x) = \frac{f^{(n+1)}(c)}{(n+1)!}(x - a)^{n+1}.</md>
          If <m>x \neq a</m>, then <m>c</m> lies strictly between <m>a</m>
          and <m>x</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          If <m>x = a</m>, then <m>T_n(a) = f(a)</m>, so
          <m>R_n(a) = 0</m> and <m>c = a</m> works. Suppose
          <m>x \neq a</m>, and let <m>J</m> be the closed interval with
          endpoints <m>a</m> and <m>x</m> (that is, <m>[a, x]</m> or
          <m>[x, a]</m>); since <m>I</m> is an interval, <m>J \subset I</m>.
          We hold <m>x</m> fixed and let the expansion point vary: for
          <m>t \in I</m>, define
          <md>F(t) = f(x) - \sum_{k=0}^{n} \frac{f^{(k)}(t)}{k!}(x - t)^k,</md>
          the error made when <m>f(x)</m> is approximated by the Taylor
          polynomial of order <m>n</m> of <m>f</m> at <m>t</m>. Then
          <m>F(x) = f(x) - f(x) = 0</m>, since every term with
          <m>k \geq 1</m> contains the factor <m>(x - x)^k = 0</m>, and
          <m>F(a) = f(x) - T_n(x) = R_n(x)</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Each <m>f^{(k)}</m> with <m>k \leq n</m> is differentiable on
          <m>I</m>, so <m>F</m> is differentiable on <m>I</m>. For
          <m>k \geq 1</m>, the product and chain rules give
          <md>\frac{d}{dt}\left[\frac{f^{(k)}(t)}{k!}(x - t)^k\right]
          = \frac{f^{(k+1)}(t)}{k!}(x - t)^k
          - \frac{f^{(k)}(t)}{(k-1)!}(x - t)^{k-1}</md>
          (for <m>k = 1</m>, read <m>(x - t)^0</m> as <m>1</m>), and for <m>k = 0</m> the derivative of <m>f(t)</m> is
          <m>f'(t)</m>. Since <m>f(x)</m> does not depend on <m>t</m>,
          <md>F'(t) = -\left[\sum_{k=0}^{n} \frac{f^{(k+1)}(t)}{k!}(x - t)^k
          - \sum_{k=1}^{n} \frac{f^{(k)}(t)}{(k-1)!}(x - t)^{k-1}\right]
          = -\frac{f^{(n+1)}(t)}{n!}(x - t)^n,</md>
          because the second sum, after the change of index
          <m>j = k - 1</m>, is the first sum without its last term.
        </p>
        <p>
          The function <m>F</m> does not satisfy the hypothesis
          <m>F(a) = F(x)</m> of Rolle's Theorem unless <m>R_n(x) = 0</m>, so
          we correct it by a multiple of a function that equals <m>1</m> at
          <m>a</m> and <m>0</m> at <m>x</m>. For <m>t \in I</m>, define
          <md>G(t) = F(t) - \left(\frac{x - t}{x - a}\right)^{n+1} F(a).</md>
          Then <m>G(a) = F(a) - F(a) = 0</m> and
          <m>G(x) = F(x) - 0 = 0</m>. The function <m>G</m> is
          differentiable on <m>I</m>, so it is continuous on <m>J</m> and
          differentiable at every point strictly between <m>a</m> and
          <m>x</m>. By Rolle's Theorem, applied on <m>J</m>, there is a
          point <m>c</m> strictly between <m>a</m> and <m>x</m> with
          <m>G'(c) = 0</m>. Since
          <md>G'(t) = -\frac{f^{(n+1)}(t)}{n!}(x - t)^n
          + \frac{(n+1)(x - t)^n}{(x - a)^{n+1}} F(a),</md>
          the equation <m>G'(c) = 0</m>, divided by <m>(x - c)^n</m> (which
          is nonzero because <m>c \neq x</m>), gives
          <md>F(a) = \frac{(x - a)^{n+1}}{(n+1) \cdot n!} f^{(n+1)}(c)
          = \frac{f^{(n+1)}(c)}{(n+1)!}(x - a)^{n+1}.</md>
          Since <m>F(a) = R_n(x)</m>, this is the claimed formula.
        </p>
      </proof>
    </theorem>
    <p>
      For <m>n = 0</m>, Taylor's Theorem gives the conclusion of
      the MVT (under slightly stronger hypotheses):
      <m>f(x) = f(a) + f'(c)(x - a)</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      You rarely know <m>c</m>. What you can use is a bound: if
      <m>|f^{(n+1)}(t)| \leq M</m> for every <m>t</m> between <m>a</m>
      and <m>x</m>, then
      <md>|f(x) - T_n(x)| \leq \frac{M}{(n+1)!}|x - a|^{n+1}.</md>
      This is how Taylor polynomials give error estimates, and it is the
      same move as bounding <m>|f'(c)|</m> in an MVT argument.
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <paragraphs xml:id="par-mod7-sg-43-cauchy">
    <title>The Cauchy Mean Value Theorem</title>
    <p>
      L'H&#xf4;pital's rule compares two functions at once. The tool
      for that is a version of the MVT for two functions.
    </p>
    <theorem xml:id="thm-mod7-cauchy-mvt">
      <statement>
        <p>
          If <m>f</m> and <m>g</m> are
          continuous on <m>[a, b]</m> and differentiable on <m>(a, b)</m>,
          then there exists <m>c \in (a, b)</m> such that
          <md>(f(b) - f(a))\, g'(c) = (g(b) - g(a))\, f'(c).</md>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          For <m>t \in [a, b]</m>, define
          <md>h(t) = (f(b) - f(a))\, g(t) - (g(b) - g(a))\, f(t).</md>
          As a combination of <m>f</m> and <m>g</m> with constant
          coefficients, <m>h</m> is continuous on <m>[a, b]</m> and
          differentiable on <m>(a, b)</m>. Expanding,
          <md>
            <mrow>h(a) \amp= f(b)g(a) - f(a)g(a) - g(b)f(a) + g(a)f(a) = f(b)g(a) - g(b)f(a),</mrow>
            <mrow>h(b) \amp= f(b)g(b) - f(a)g(b) - g(b)f(b) + g(a)f(b) = f(b)g(a) - g(b)f(a),</mrow>
          </md>
          so <m>h(a) = h(b)</m>. By Rolle's Theorem, there is
          <m>c \in (a, b)</m> with
          <m>0 = h'(c) = (f(b) - f(a))\, g'(c) - (g(b) - g(a))\, f'(c)</m>.
        </p>
      </proof>
    </theorem>
    <p>
      Taking <m>g(t) = t</m> recovers the MVT. If in addition
      <m>g'(t) \neq 0</m> for every <m>t \in (a, b)</m>, then
      <m>g(b) \neq g(a)</m> (otherwise Rolle's Theorem applied to
      <m>g</m> would give a point where <m>g'</m> is <m>0</m>), and the
      conclusion can be written as a ratio:
      <md>\frac{f(b) - f(a)}{g(b) - g(a)} = \frac{f'(c)}{g'(c)}.</md>
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <paragraphs xml:id="par-mod7-sg-43-lhopital">
    <title>L'H&#xf4;pital's Rule</title>
    <theorem xml:id="thm-mod7-lhopital">
      <title>L'H&#xf4;pital's Rule (the <m>0/0</m> Form at a Point)</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>a \in \R</m>, and suppose there is an open interval
          <m>I</m> containing <m>a</m> such that
          <ol marker="(i)">
            <li>
              <m>f</m> and <m>g</m> are differentiable at every
              <m>x \in I</m> with <m>x \neq a</m>;
            </li>
            <li>
              <m>g'(x) \neq 0</m> for every <m>x \in I</m> with
              <m>x \neq a</m>;
            </li>
            <li>
              <m>\lim_{x \to a} f(x) = 0</m> and
              <m>\lim_{x \to a} g(x) = 0</m>;
            </li>
            <li>
              <m>\lim_{x \to a} f'(x)/g'(x) = L</m> for some
              <m>L \in \R</m>.
            </li>
          </ol>
          Then <m>g(x) \neq 0</m> for every <m>x \in I</m> with
          <m>x \neq a</m>, and
          <md>\lim_{x \to a} \frac{f(x)}{g(x)} = L.</md>
          Nothing is assumed about <m>f</m> or <m>g</m> at <m>a</m> itself;
          they need not be defined there.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          Define <m>\varphi(x) = f(x)</m> and
          <m>\psi(x) = g(x)</m> for <m>x \in I</m> with <m>x \neq a</m>, and
          <m>\varphi(a) = \psi(a) = 0</m>. Then <m>\varphi</m> and <m>\psi</m> are
          continuous on <m>I</m>: at a point <m>x \neq a</m> because they
          agree with <m>f</m> and <m>g</m> on an open interval around
          <m>x</m>, where <m>f</m> and <m>g</m> are differentiable and
          hence continuous (<xref ref="ex-mod7-sg-41-2"/>), and at <m>a</m>
          by (iii). Fix <m>x \in I</m> with <m>x \neq a</m>, and let
          <m>K</m> be the closed interval with endpoints <m>a</m> and
          <m>x</m>; then <m>K \subset I</m>, and <m>\varphi</m> and <m>\psi</m> are
          continuous on <m>K</m> and differentiable at every point strictly
          between <m>a</m> and <m>x</m>, with <m>\varphi' = f'</m> and
          <m>\psi' = g'</m> there.
        </p>
        <p>
          First, <m>g(x) \neq 0</m>: otherwise <m>\psi(x) = 0 = \psi(a)</m>, and
          Rolle's Theorem applied to <m>\psi</m> on <m>K</m> would give a point
          strictly between <m>a</m> and <m>x</m> where <m>g'</m> is
          <m>0</m>, contrary to (ii). Next, the Cauchy Mean Value Theorem
          (<xref ref="thm-mod7-cauchy-mvt"/>) applied to <m>\varphi</m> and <m>\psi</m> on <m>K</m> gives a point
          <m>c_x</m> strictly between <m>a</m> and <m>x</m> with
          <md>\varphi(x)\, \psi'(c_x) = \psi(x)\, \varphi'(c_x), \quad \text{that is,} \quad
          f(x)\, g'(c_x) = g(x)\, f'(c_x).</md>
          (When <m>x \lt a</m>, the theorem on <m>[x, a]</m> gives
          <m>(\varphi(a) - \varphi(x))\, \psi'(c_x) = (\psi(a) - \psi(x))\, \varphi'(c_x)</m>, which
          is the same equation multiplied by <m>-1</m>.) Dividing by
          <m>g(x)\, g'(c_x)</m>, which is nonzero by (ii) and the first
          step,
          <md>\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} = \frac{f'(c_x)}{g'(c_x)}.</md>
        </p>
        <p>
          Now let <m>\varepsilon \gt 0</m>. By (iv), and because <m>I</m>
          is open, we can choose <m>\delta \gt 0</m> small enough that
          <m>(a - \delta, a + \delta) \subset I</m> and
          <m>|f'(t)/g'(t) - L| \lt \varepsilon</m> whenever
          <m>0 \lt |t - a| \lt \delta</m>. If
          <m>0 \lt |x - a| \lt \delta</m>, then <m>c_x</m> lies strictly
          between <m>a</m> and <m>x</m>, so
          <m>0 \lt |c_x - a| \lt |x - a| \lt \delta</m>, and therefore
          <md>\left|\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} - L\right|
          = \left|\frac{f'(c_x)}{g'(c_x)} - L\right| \lt \varepsilon.</md>
          Hence <m>\lim_{x \to a} f(x)/g(x) = L</m>.
        </p>
      </proof>
    </theorem>
    <p>
      <xref ref="thm-mod7-lhopital"/> is the only form of the rule this
      module needs.
      Notice the direction of the argument: hypothesis (iv) asks you to
      know that <m>f'/g'</m> has a limit <em>before</em> the rule says
      anything about <m>f/g</m>. If <m>f'/g'</m> has no limit, the rule
      is silent, and <m>f/g</m> may still converge. For example, take
      <m>f(x) = x^2 \sin(1/x)</m> and <m>g(x) = x</m> for
      <m>x \neq 0</m>, with <m>a = 0</m>. Hypotheses (i) to (iii) hold,
      and <m>f(x)/g(x) = x \sin(1/x) \to 0</m> because
      <m>|x \sin(1/x)| \leq |x|</m>. But
      <m>f'(x)/g'(x) = 2x\sin(1/x) - \cos(1/x)</m> has no limit as
      <m>x \to 0</m>. If it tended to some <m>M</m>, then, since
      <m>2x \sin(1/x) \to 0</m> (because
      <m>|2x \sin(1/x)| \leq 2|x|</m>), the difference
      <m>\cos(1/x) = 2x \sin(1/x) - f'(x)/g'(x)</m> would tend to
      <m>-M</m>, yet <m>\cos(1/x)</m> equals <m>1</m> at the points
      <m>1/(2\pi n)</m> and <m>-1</m> at the points
      <m>1/((2n+1)\pi)</m>, both of which tend to <m>0</m> (compare
      <xref ref="ex-mod6-sg-31-3"/>). When you apply the rule twice,
      the same direction applies: you must show that the second ratio of
      derivatives has a limit before you can use the rule on the first.
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

</section>
